# Introduction to Applying Changes To Multiple Namespaces At Once

Users are prevented from submitting PRs which will affect multiple namespaces at once. Occasionally we will need to do it e.g. during incidents, or at user request if they have a very large amount of namespaces to update.

## Pre-requisites

- A PR with all the updates in it (typically created by the user). This PR will fail on the `reject-multi-namespace-prs.yml` check.
- Access to concourse
- Access to [cloud-platform-terraform-concourse](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-concourse)
- If running locally: [fly](https://concourse-ci.org/docs/fly/) installed

## Process

When a change needs to be applied to multiple namespaces in a single operation, use the following process.

1. Consider whether the user has appropriate permissions for what they are requesting. This is needed as the `rbac-permissions-check` will only check one namespace.

2. Obtain a list of all namespaces that need to be updated. The `github-actions` bot should have commented a list on the PR.

3. Dismiss the failing `reject-multi-namespace-prs.yml` check on the PR.

4. Create a Concourse pipeline to run the apply. This can be done locally with `fly` or by updating `cloud-platform-terraform-concourse`. See the relevant section below.

5. Ensure no other changes are being applied to the affected namespaces at the same time.
   - Running multiple Terraform applies against the same namespace concurrently may result in state lock contention or failed applies.
   - If this cannot be assured, consider temporarily pausing the [environments-live](https://concourse.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/teams/main/pipelines/environments-live/) pipeline until the multi-namespace apply has completed.

6. Merge the pull request.

7. Trigger the multi-namespace apply job:
   - Navigate to:
     - https://concourse.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/teams/main/pipelines/multi-ns-applier/jobs/apply-namespaces/builds/
   - Unpause this pipeline
   - Start a new build and monitor it until completion.

8. Cleanup:
   - If you modified the shared `multi-ns-applier.yaml` file, remove the temporary namespace entries once the change has been successfully applied and merge the cleanup change into `main`. This reduces the risk of future executions unintentionally targeting the same namespaces.
   - Pause the bootstrap pipeline if you unpaused it.

## Adding The Pipeline To Concourse

### Adding The Pipeline To The Repository

Pick this option if you want the applier pipeline to persist.

1. Update the multi-namespace applier pipeline configuration:
   - Add the affected namespaces to the following file (you may need to create it):
     - https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-concourse/blob/main/pipelines/manager/main/multi-ns-applier.yaml
   - Raise and merge a pull request into `main`.
   - Note that you don't need to cut a release, the job just needs to be in `main`. The bootstrap pipeline will look at all the files in `../pipelines/manager/main` directory and the tile for job will appear.

2. Unpause the bootstrap pipeline in the Concourse CI.

3. Now continue the Process section at Step 5.

### Running the Pipeline Locally With `fly`

Instead of updating the shared pipeline configuration, you can create a temporary copy of the pipeline and execute it using `fly`. Pick this option for one-off applies.

For example:

1. Clone a local copy of [cloud-platform-terraform-concourse](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-concourse)

2. Update `pipelines/disabled/multi-ns-applier.yaml`

3. Create the pipeline:
   - `fly -t manager set-pipeline -p multi-ns-applier-[some-name] -c pipelines/disabled/multi-ns-applier.yaml`
   - If you are not already authenticated, `fly` will display an error similar to:

     ```text
     could not find a valid token.
     logging in to team 'main'
     navigate to the following URL in your browser:
     https://concourse.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/login?fly_port=XXXX
     ```

   - Open the URL in your browser and complete the authentication process.
   - Re-run the `fly set-pipeline` command.

4. Now continue the Process section at Step 5.
